5:[["$","$L10","4.6",{"topic":{"id":"4.6","title":"Recovery of Deleted Files and Artifacts","minutes":14,"summary":"Deleted, hidden, and inaccessible are different states. Recovery depends on storage behavior, filesystem context, and encryption.","sections":[{"title":"What deletion actually changes","body":["A filesystem may mark an entry as unused while leaving some content or metadata recoverable. Subsequent writes, journal behavior, cleanup, and application maintenance can change that state. On flash storage, TRIM and garbage collection may erase underlying pages independently of the visible filesystem operation. Do not promise recovery merely because deletion was recent.","Hidden files can be ordinary application data, protected system files, or intentionally concealed material. A hidden attribute is not proof of wrongdoing. Alternate data streams, unusual extensions, archive containers, and cloud placeholders require format-aware examination rather than a single “show hidden files” action."]},{"title":"Filesystem recovery and carving","body":["Filesystem-aware recovery uses surviving entries and metadata, which can preserve names and paths. Signature-based carving searches for recognizable file structures when those entries are unavailable. Fragmentation, embedded files, encryption, and damaged headers complicate carving. A carved file may be incomplete or combine unrelated fragments if the method is unsuitable.","Work from the preserved image and record the tool, version, settings, source ranges, and recovered output hashes. Validate important results by opening them in a safe viewer or checking internal structure. Do not execute recovered programs. Compare with backups, cloud versions, or message attachments where lawful and available."]},{"title":"Explain failures and attribution limits","body":["A recovered file’s presence in unallocated space does not prove who created, used, or deleted it. Seek corroborating application artifacts, account records, and independent context. Explain whether timestamps came from surviving metadata, embedded content, or tool assumptions. Do not silently restore a filename that the recovery method could not actually establish.","Record failed recovery and its likely causes without overstating them. “The tool did not recover a valid file” is different from “no such file ever existed”. A careful negative finding can guide further collection, such as a provider export or backup request, without turning absence into proof."]}],"practice":{"title":"Choose a recovery route","scenario":"An encrypted SSD image contains a deleted-entry reference but no readable file content.","steps":["Distinguish metadata recovery from content recovery.","Identify encryption, TRIM, and overwriting as possible limits.","Check lawful backups or cloud versions rather than forcing a result.","Document the failed method and the evidence still available."],"debrief":"Recovery is conditional. Understanding why a method failed is part of a defensible examination."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide"],"labId":"hash","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain what deletion actually changes and apply it to an investigation.","Explain filesystem recovery and carving and apply it to an investigation.","Explain explain failures and attribution limits and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.6","prompt":"Which factor can make deleted-data recovery from an SSD difficult?","options":["The screen brightness.","TRIM and flash garbage collection.","The desktop wallpaper alone.","The number of folders in the user interface."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Flash-management behavior can erase or relocate underlying data independently of filesystem expectations.","unit":4}}}]]
